WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.608 --> 00:00:06.099
- A hundred years ago,
archaeology was for the rich.

00:00:06.099 --> 00:00:08.566
50 years ago, it was for men,

00:00:08.566 --> 00:00:11.980
but we are expanding it to the world.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.854
I wish for us to discover the millions

00:00:15.854 --> 00:00:19.482
of unknown archaeological
sites around the world,

00:00:19.482 --> 00:00:23.752
by creating a 21st century
army of global explorers.

00:00:23.752 --> 00:00:28.752
We'll find and protect the
world's hidden heritage.

00:00:28.796 --> 00:00:31.062
So, how are we going to do this?

00:00:31.062 --> 00:00:33.276
(soft music)

00:00:33.276 --> 00:00:36.776
- They had a big idea to change the world,

00:00:37.653 --> 00:00:39.842
but they couldn't do it alone.

00:00:39.842 --> 00:00:40.748
- So, my wish-

00:00:40.748 --> 00:00:41.900
- For my wish-

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.165
- My wish-
- So, my wish-

00:00:43.165 --> 00:00:45.332
- And now, here's my wish.

00:00:53.104 --> 00:00:55.388
- I am an archaeologist, and I specialize

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:59.853
in using satellite imagery
to map ancient landscapes.

00:00:59.853 --> 00:01:02.377
The really fun title is space archaeology.

00:01:02.377 --> 00:01:04.924
You, of course, maybe wanna record
how thick something is,

00:01:04.924 --> 00:01:06.098
which you can't measure from space,

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:08.063
whether that's soil, whether that's snow.

00:01:08.063 --> 00:01:09.758
When I won the prize,
I had to present this

00:01:09.758 --> 00:01:11.591
completely crazy wish.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.823
I had to present the case
for archaeology to the world.

00:01:15.823 --> 00:01:16.921
Why does it matter?

00:01:16.921 --> 00:01:17.754
So what?

00:01:19.391 --> 00:01:21.079
The reality is there aren't
enough of us scientists.

00:01:21.079 --> 00:01:23.174
We've got to give more
people opportunities

00:01:23.174 --> 00:01:24.841
to become explorers.

00:01:28.022 --> 00:01:30.261
(paper shuffling)

00:01:30.261 --> 00:01:34.133
GlobalXplorer is an online
crowd-sourcing platform

00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:37.959
that allows anyone in the
world to help map ancient sites

00:01:37.959 --> 00:01:40.321
by looking at satellite imagery.

00:01:40.321 --> 00:01:43.229
And it's this idea that everyone in the world

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:45.782
can make a contribution to science.

00:01:45.782 --> 00:01:47.199
It can be anyone.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.217
- I'm Doris Jones.

00:01:54.217 --> 00:01:55.233
I'm 90 years old.

00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:59.171
I live alone, except for my two kitties.

00:01:59.171 --> 00:02:00.644
And I need something to do.

00:02:00.644 --> 00:02:04.338
I play games; I like Sudoku.

00:02:04.338 --> 00:02:07.505
I play Mahjong with friends on Friday.

00:02:08.503 --> 00:02:10.639
(clinking)

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:13.056
And I'm an armchair archaeologist.

00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:19.242
Why don't you wanna work?

00:02:19.242 --> 00:02:20.825
Down,

00:02:20.825 --> 00:02:22.242
down, let's work.

00:02:23.335 --> 00:02:25.597
- Doris is one of our top contributors.

00:02:25.597 --> 00:02:27.259
She's a space archaeologist.

00:02:27.259 --> 00:02:30.841
She's looked at many, many,
many thousands of tiles.

00:02:30.841 --> 00:02:33.775
People like her are just
a gift to GlobalXplorer.

00:02:33.775 --> 00:02:35.466
- Right now, I'm running out of tile,

00:02:35.466 --> 00:02:39.959
but well, it has a wall probably for animals.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:44.626
But it has a number of what look
more like trails than roads.

00:02:46.476 --> 00:02:47.959
I'll go to the next one.

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:50.371
- All you have to do is look at an image,

00:02:50.371 --> 00:02:51.269
and look carefully,

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:53.755
and you see that there could potentially

00:02:53.755 --> 00:02:55.422
be a new site there.

00:02:58.495 --> 00:03:02.213
Doris just has this passion and energy

00:03:02.213 --> 00:03:04.100
and excitement for exploration.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.864
You know, she's able to give back
and really contribute

00:03:05.864 --> 00:03:08.055
in a meaningful way.

00:03:08.055 --> 00:03:10.722
(Skype ringing)

00:03:13.097 --> 00:03:14.301
- Doris!

00:03:14.301 --> 00:03:15.229
- Hi!

00:03:15.229 --> 00:03:17.724
- I'm actually crying right
now because I'm, like,

00:03:17.724 --> 00:03:19.954
overwhelmed with emotion.

00:03:19.954 --> 00:03:21.454
- Well, thank you.
- To just see your face and say, “Thank you.”

00:03:22.306 --> 00:03:25.643
I just really admire what you're doing.

00:03:25.643 --> 00:03:27.924
And I really enjoyed the program.

00:03:27.924 --> 00:03:31.066
- When did your love for
archaeologist and paleontology

00:03:31.066 --> 00:03:32.616
and science start?

00:03:32.616 --> 00:03:35.071
- Well, in the middle ’50s,

00:03:35.071 --> 00:03:39.102
my husband and I were in the
backyard, digging a garden.

00:03:39.102 --> 00:03:42.724
It was late fall; and we were
getting ready for next spring.

00:03:42.724 --> 00:03:46.621
And I saw a strange looking
white rock on the ground.

00:03:46.621 --> 00:03:48.610
I picked it up, and he said,

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:49.933
"Let me see that."

00:03:49.933 --> 00:03:50.965
I showed it to him and he said,

00:03:50.965 --> 00:03:51.996
"You know what this is?"

00:03:51.996 --> 00:03:53.554
I told him I didn't.

00:03:53.554 --> 00:03:56.111
He said, "This is a fossil."

00:03:56.111 --> 00:03:57.831
Then we took out to the creek

00:03:57.831 --> 00:04:00.061
and started looking for fossils.

00:04:00.061 --> 00:04:03.472
From then on, there was no
turning back. (laughing)

00:04:03.472 --> 00:04:05.268
- That's wonderful.

00:04:05.268 --> 00:04:08.018
- So, then I collected for years.

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:11.947
After all those years, of course,

00:04:11.947 --> 00:04:14.902
I've got all those memories.

00:04:14.902 --> 00:04:18.579
That a type of thing- it's
not just hunting the fossils,

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:19.966
but the experiences.

00:04:19.966 --> 00:04:23.799
And you just learn and really
just live and enjoy.

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:27.454
And I think the fun is in the hunt.

00:04:27.454 --> 00:04:30.849
You know, it's like, well,
like the rainbow, you know.

00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:32.861
The end of the rainbow's a pot of gold.

00:04:32.861 --> 00:04:36.361
So, GlobalXplorer was kind of my rainbow.

00:04:39.755 --> 00:04:43.201
And even in my age, I can continue to hunt

00:04:43.201 --> 00:04:45.118
for lost civilizations.

00:04:46.454 --> 00:04:48.845
- I want to tell you about
some of the incredible things

00:04:48.845 --> 00:04:51.170
that have happened as a
result of your discoveries.

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:54.489
We're now beginning to
take thousands of features

00:04:54.489 --> 00:04:56.038
that people like you have found,

00:04:56.038 --> 00:05:00.955
and we're sharing them with
archaeologist who work in Peru.

00:05:01.955 --> 00:05:05.714
They're beginning to confirm
the things the crowd has found.

00:05:05.714 --> 00:05:09.108
So, in other words, the platform works.

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:10.945
Ultimately, you know, this technology is helping us

00:05:10.945 --> 00:05:12.443
to rewrite our history.

00:05:12.443 --> 00:05:14.256
- We need to know our history.

00:05:14.256 --> 00:05:16.089
We're all one peoples.

00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:19.072
It's all our history;
we're all here on this

00:05:19.072 --> 00:05:21.495
little ball of dirt.

00:05:21.495 --> 00:05:24.114
- I believe we have barely
scratched the surface

00:05:24.114 --> 00:05:27.114
in terms of what's left to discover.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.823
And the greatest story ever told is the story

00:05:30.823 --> 00:05:33.154
of our shared human journey,

00:05:33.154 --> 00:05:36.232
but the only way that we're
going to be able to write it

00:05:36.232 --> 00:05:38.982
is if we do it together.

